                1.1,
                0.008,
                0.008
              ],
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "newscript",
                  "scriptName": "retrograde_arc_renderer",
                  "sciptDescription": "Draws a curved arc line (using a LineRenderer) representing the retrograde loop path of Mars across the starfield backdrop. Accepts a start point, a peak offset (how high the loop arcs), and an end point, all as Vector3 inspector fields. Renders a smooth Bezier-style curve with configurable color and line width. Does not require physics or user interaction."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "NightSkyNarratorPanel",
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Apparent Retrograde Motion \u2014 Mars moves westward for ~2 months",
                  "color": [
                    255,
                    120,
                    80,
                    255
                  ],
                  "fontSize": 26,
                  "wrapText": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Mars moves westward back toward Leo, and the trail object expands to show the full retrograde loop arc. The narrator panel updates to label the retrograde phase. A looping path line makes the arc visible to students.",
          "narration": "Over the next two months, Mars traces a westward loop across the sky. This backward drift is called apparent retrograde motion. Notice how the path curves \u2014 Mars isn't moving in a straight line but describes a loop before eventually reversing again and resuming its eastward journey."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip3",
          "audioClip": "nightsky_resumes_eastward_geocentric_puzzle",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "MarsNightSky",
              "position": [
                0.35,
                1.52,
                1.1
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "NightSkyNarratorPanel",
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Mars resumes eastward drift. Geocentric model needed 'epicycles' to explain this loop.",
                  "color": [
                    200,
                    255,
                    180,
                    255
                  ],
                  "fontSize": 26,
                  "wrapText": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Mars moves back to an eastward position in Virgo, completing the loop. The full trail now shows the iconic retrograde loop shape. The narrator panel summarizes the geocentric epicycle problem and previews the next module.",
          "narration": "Eventually Mars reaches its second stationary point, then resumes its normal eastward drift. The entire retrograde episode \u2014 from first slow-down to full resumption \u2014 spans roughly 2 months. To ancient geocentric astronomers, explaining this loop required inventing a complex mechanism called an epicycle. In the next scene, we'll see how the heliocentric model makes the puzzle disappear."
        }
